<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bocchus II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 33 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bocchus II was king of Mauretania, ruling its eastern part from around 49 BCE and the whole kingdom after expelling his brother Bogud in 38 BCE. He supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and then Octavian in the Roman civil wars. When he died in 33 BCE without heirs he left Mauretania to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and in 25 BCE Augustus gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bocchus-ii.jpg" alt="Bocchus II" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG, (uploaded by Aṭlas) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bocchus_II_%C3%86_77000344.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bocchus II was the son of Mastanesosus, king of Mauretania, who died in 49 BCE. His parentage is known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> legends of his coins, which read Rex Bocchus Sosi f, King Bocchus son of Sosus. By 49 BCE Mauretania appears to have been shared between Bocchus and Bogud. Bocchus held the eastern part with its capital at Iol, Bogud the western part with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Only three events of his reign are known: his dealings with Sittius, his part in the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a> and the Pompeians, and his annexation of western Mauretania. It is not known exactly when he began to rule, but he had been hostile to Juba before the Caesarean Senate recognised him as king in 49 BCE, and he and Bogud took Caesar's side against the Pompeians and their ally Juba I, king of Numidia.</p><p>During the Roman civil war of 49–45 BCE Bocchus and Sittius invaded Numidia, overthrew Massinissa II and within a few days took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, Juba's capital, so that Juba had to give up his plan of joining Metellus Scipio against Caesar. Bocchus went no further, but Sittius fought on. At the end of the war Caesar gave Bocchus part of Massinissa II's lands, which extended Mauretania to the Ampsaga; Massinissa's son Arabio recovered them after Caesar's murder.</p><p>After Caesar's death Bocchus sided with Octavian and Bogud with Antony. While Bogud was away in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Bocchus seized the whole of Mauretania and was confirmed as sole ruler by Octavian, uniting a larger Mauretanian kingdom than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-i">Bocchus I</a> or Sosus had ruled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bocchus died without an heir in 33 BCE and left his kingdom to Augustus. After a period of direct Roman administration from 33 to 25 BCE, Augustus gave Mauretania to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a>. Enlarged by western Numidia, it remained a Roman client kingdom under Juba II and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-of-mauretania">Ptolemy of Mauretania</a> until it was annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bocchus II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After <b>Bocchus II</b> bequeathed the kingdom to Rome, Augustus installed Juba II in 25 BCE, and Juba married Cleopatra Selene, daughter of Cleopatra VII.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a></li></ul></section>
